Gaslight
========

Flaring analysis for the Permian Basin of West Texas. Gaslight
cross-references satellite-detected flares with public state records —
permits, wells, leases, self-reported flaring volumes, and methane
plume observations — so you can see where gas is being burned and
compare it against what operators tell regulators.

  VIIRS Nightfire (satellite flare detections)
  + Sentinel-2 SWIR flare catalogue (from permian-flaring)
  + SWR 32 flaring/venting exception permits (Texas RRC)
  + Wells, leases, operators, gatherers (RRC EBCDIC bulk files)
  + Self-reported flared volumes (RRC PDQ production data)
  + RRC R-3 gas processing facilities
  + Carbon Mapper / UNEP IMEO methane plumes
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  → dist/gaslight.duckdb  (the shareable dataset)
  → research.datadesk.eco/gaslight/  (the interactive map)


The Data
========

Two ways in, both built from the same pipeline.

1. The dataset: dist/gaslight.duckdb
------------------------------------

The product is a single, standalone DuckDB file:

  dist/gaslight.duckdb

It is self-contained — one plain `main` schema, no internal staging,
no geometry blobs. Latitude/longitude are kept as decimal-degree
columns (WGS84), so every table reads cleanly into pandas, R, QGIS,
or plain SQL. This is the one file to share and analyse.

Open it with the free DuckDB CLI:

  duckdb dist/gaslight.duckdb

  -- list tables
  SHOW TABLES;
  -- the brightest flare sites by total radiant heat
  SELECT flare_id, lat, lon, detection_days, total_rh_mw
  FROM flare_sites ORDER BY total_rh_mw DESC LIMIT 20;

Or from Python (`pip install duckdb`):

  import duckdb
  con = duckdb.connect("dist/gaslight.duckdb")
  df = con.sql("SELECT * FROM flare_sites").df()   # straight to pandas

R users can read it via the duckdb package; QGIS can load any table
through its latitude/longitude columns.

Tables:

  flare_sites       VIIRS-observed flare locations + how active each is
  flare_detections  per-flare-site, per-night detection time series
  permits           RRC SWR 32 flaring/venting exception permits
  permit_leases     leases underlying each permit filing
  wells             every Permian well, w/ operator + lease flaring metrics
  leases            lease-level rollup of reported flaring + intensity
  monthly_flaring   monthly lease-level gas flared/vented + produced
  gatherers         gatherers, purchasers, nominators per lease
  plumes            methane plume detections (Carbon Mapper + IMEO)
  facilities        RRC R-3 gas processing facilities (gas plants)
  operators         RRC operator number → name lookup
  s2_detections     Sentinel-2 SWIR flare catalogue (see below)

Read this before comparing volumes: the flaring VOLUMES
(monthly_flaring, and the flared_mcf / flaring_intensity_pct columns)
are SELF-REPORTED by operators to the RRC. The satellite layers
(flare_sites, flare_detections, s2_detections) are INDEPENDENT
observations from space. They do not always agree — and that gap is
the story. Treat the two families of evidence as separate, not
interchangeable.

Every table, column, and source is documented in the data dictionary:

  docs/data-dictionary/      (browsable markdown, start at README.md)

The same content is embedded inside the database itself, so you can
query it without leaving SQL:

  SELECT * FROM _dictionary WHERE table_name = 'wells';
  SELECT * FROM _sources;

2. The interactive map
----------------------

  http://research.datadesk.eco/gaslight/

A live MapLibre map of the same data — a flare-centric subset
projected to parquet files and queried in your browser with DuckDB
WASM. Click a flare to see its detail card, nearby permits and wells,
reported volumes, and Sentinel-2 history. Zero install; deep links in
the URL hash for sharing a view or a specific flare.


How It Works
============

For each VIIRS-detected flare, gaslight assembles CONTEXT, not a
verdict. It lists:

  - permitted flares within 375m (the VIIRS M-band pixel radius),
  - wells and the leases they belong to within 375m,
  - R-3 gas processing plants within 5km.

It deliberately does NOT name a single operator with a confidence
score. Proximity inside a single satellite pixel is not ownership, and
forcing an attribution produced too many wrong calls. The card shows
who and what is nearby; the reader judges.

Leases are the bridge to volumes: wells carry their lease district and
number from RRC records, and grouping by those fields connects a flare
to its lease's self-reported flaring from RRC PDQ production data.

Methane plumes (Carbon Mapper + UNEP IMEO) are overlaid spatially for
cross-reference.

Scope: everything is clipped to the Permian bounding box
(30.0–33.5°N, -104.5 to -100.0°W; above 32°N, east of -103.064°W to
stay in Texas). Time-series layers cover 2021 onward. Matching uses a
fixed 375m radius. Nothing is imputed — missing values stay missing.


The Sentinel-2 layer
====================

Gaslight runs NO Sentinel-2 detection of its own. The S2 flare layer
is a score-capped catalogue of flare sites CONSUMED FROM THE SIBLING
permian-flaring REPOSITORY — that project is the Sentinel-2 detection
engine and the methods paper.

Gaslight pulls the catalogue with a fetch step:

  make s2     # writes data/s2_catalogue.parquet from permian-flaring

It then flows through the normal pipeline into the s2_detections
table — one row per H3 site, with each date's observations embedded as
a `detections` JSON array. The S2 layer covers 2025–2026 (permian-
flaring's S2 analysis window); the other layers are 2021+.


Rebuilding It
=============

Setup:

  uv sync
  uv run playwright install chromium    # only needed for RRC downloads

Requires: uv and the duckdb CLI. Playwright/Chromium is only used by
the RRC bulk-file downloader.

Pipeline (load → rrc → publish → export):

  make refresh    Rebuild the whole database from scratch
  make db         Full pipeline: load → rrc → publish → export
  make publish    Build the shareable dist/gaslight.duckdb + dictionary
  make export     Re-export the web app's parquets
  make serve      Local map on :8080
  make help       List all targets

The SQL pipeline lives in queries/:

  load.sql     faithful raw load of the source files
  rrc.sql      normalised Texas oil & gas foundation tables
  publish.sql  builds the clean permian.* tables + dist/gaslight.duckdb
  export.sql   projects them to web/data/*.parquet for the map

Fetchers / scrapers (in scripts/, driven by make targets):

  make permits          SWR 32 permit metadata
  make permit-details   permit detail pages → CSVs
  make wells            RRC EBCDIC well + operator records
  make vnf              VIIRS Nightfire profiles (EOG)
  make plumes           Carbon Mapper + IMEO methane plumes
  make r3               RRC R-3 gas processing facilities
  make s2               permian-flaring's S2 catalogue

  duckdb data/data.duckdb   interactive queries on the working DB


Project Structure
=================

  scripts/   scrapers, parsers, fetchers (RRC, VNF, plumes, R-3, S2)
  queries/   SQL pipeline (load → rrc → publish → export)
  docs/      data dictionary (docs/data-dictionary/)
  data/      working DB, CSVs, parquets (gitignored)
  dist/      dist/gaslight.duckdb — the shareable product
  web/       interactive map (MapLibre GL + DuckDB WASM)
    app.js     map setup, detail panels, shared helpers
    db.js      DuckDB WASM queries + operator attribution
    drawer.js  tabbed data drawer with sort + keyboard nav
    s2.js      Sentinel-2 catalogue layer + detail cards
    style.css  styling (CSS custom properties)
    vendor/    vendored deps (maplibre-gl, duckdb-wasm, fonts)
    data/      exported parquets the map loads

The web app has zero npm dependencies and no build step — MapLibre GL
and DuckDB WASM are vendored, and everything runs client-side.


Deployment
==========

  make vendor     Download vendored JS deps (one-off / on update)
  make export     Re-export the web parquets

Push to main to deploy via GitHub Pages.
